WEBVTT

00:00:05.545 --> 00:00:07.140
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> Hi, I’m Karen.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:09.740
Welcome to my home town, Manchester.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:23.540
Manchester is a city in the north west of England.

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:31.540
When you think of Manchester, you probably think of our football teams,

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.900
but there is a lot more to the city.

00:00:34.731 --> 00:00:38.180
This is the Manchester Museum of Science and Industry.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:39.912
I’ve come here today,

00:00:39.913 --> 00:00:43.780
because I’m going to find out about the city’s link to the British weekend.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:48.700
Like many good ideas, it started right here in Manchester.

00:00:51.075 --> 00:00:56.420
To us, the weekend is part of our routine and we can’t imagine life without it.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.500
But, there was a time when working people didn’t really get a weekend.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.880
In the nineteenth century,

00:01:08.881 --> 00:01:13.660
thousands of people left their homes in the country to find work in the city.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.900
Manchester became the first industrial city and it was the home of the cotton industry.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:31.755
Until 1843, people worked for six days – from Monday to Saturday – and had a day of rest on Sunday,

00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:33.300
when they went to church.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:40.740
The work – like here in a cotton mill – was hard and often dangerous.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:46.440
But in the 1840s, a group of men began to ask local factory owners

00:01:46.441 --> 00:01:50.300
to give people an extra half day off on Saturday afternoon.

00:01:51.097 --> 00:01:57.860
One of these men, Robert Lowes, was the great, great grandfather of the British actor, Sir Ian McKellen.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.700
Lowes and his colleagues had three reasons for this change.

00:02:03.038 --> 00:02:05.020
The first reason was commercial.

00:02:05.385 --> 00:02:07.915
When people worked from Monday to Saturday,

00:02:08.086 --> 00:02:12.220
they had no time to spend their money, because shops were shut on Sunday.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.060
The second reason was religious.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.620
People were tired from the working week, so they didn’t always go to church on Sundays.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:23.140
The last reason was economic.

00:02:23.406 --> 00:02:27.281
When people had more time off, they could rest more, be healthier,

00:02:27.404 --> 00:02:29.488
and work harder during the week.

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:36.352
In 1843, Lowes and his colleagues persuaded many of Manchester’s factory owners

00:02:36.353 --> 00:02:39.598
to close their factories at two o’clock on Saturday.

00:02:41.808 --> 00:02:46.940
By the 1870s, all workers had the same one-and-a-half-day weekend.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:52.860
The extra half day made a big difference to people and to society.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:03:00.020
With more free time, people could relax, shop, and watch and play sports.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.507
In fact, Manchester’s love of football began at this time;

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:12.470
in 1878 Newton Heath football club began and two years later St Mark’s Football Club was formed.

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:16.380
Newton Heath became Manchester United.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:20.020
And St Mark’s became Manchester City.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:27.980
The British weekend has continued to change and, by the 1960s, we had a two-day weekend.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:37.060
Today we enjoy a very different weekend to our ancestors.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.780
We now watch and play sports.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:46.340
We go to the cinema.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:49.620
We eat out in restaurants.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:54.860
Or we go to shopping centres like this one – the Arndale Centre –

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:01.900
because in the 1990s the law changed and shops started to open on Sundays.

00:04:03.606 --> 00:04:06.580
But all these changes haven’t been good for everyone.

00:04:07.876 --> 00:04:10.782
Some people, shop assistants, for example,

00:04:11.264 --> 00:04:14.780
have to work at the weekend and are not paid any extra.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:21.660
Maybe our working week will change again to improve our quality of life.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:29.137
One suggestion is that we make the working week four days and increase the weekend to three days.

00:04:30.299 --> 00:04:35.329
In theory, this would be better for the economy, because more people would have a job,

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:39.380
and we would also spend more time with our families.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:48.380
Seventy-one per cent of British workers think Britain would be a happier place with a three-day weekend.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:53.620
Until that happens, have a good weekend – a two-day weekend!